WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.731
Begin by framing Process Model & State as a
question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:07.731 --> 00:00:08.828
observable results.

3
00:00:08.828 --> 00:00:15.631
A process combines an address space with
execution state and operating-system

4
00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:16.250
resources.

5
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:24.095
Consider this worked case: A running web
server process maintains its own stack, heap,

6
00:00:24.095 --> 00:00:28.578
and open file descriptors while executing
user-level code.

7
00:00:28.578 --> 00:00:33.249
Annotate each component with its function in
the OS model.

8
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:40.293
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

9
00:00:40.293 --> 00:00:40.796
conclusion.

10
00:00:40.796 --> 00:00:46.953
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

11
00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:54.655
Now define System Calls & Privilege Levels
precisely enough that another learner could

12
00:00:54.655 --> 00:00:56.406
test the definition.

13
00:00:56.406 --> 00:01:02.766
A system call transfers control from user code
to a privileged kernel service.

14
00:01:02.766 --> 00:01:10.320
Consider this worked case: When a program
requests disk I/O, it executes a system call

15
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.859
instruction that switches execution to the
kernel to handle the request.

16
00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:21.898
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:21.898 --> 00:01:24.094
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:24.094 --> 00:01:30.609
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:38.233
With the foundation in place, examine how
Traps & Processor State operates in a concrete

20
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:38.734
sequence.

21
00:01:38.734 --> 00:01:46.263
A trap records processor state so the kernel
can handle a system call, exception, or

22
00:01:46.263 --> 00:01:46.765
interrupt.

23
00:01:46.765 --> 00:01:53.976
Consider this worked case: When a page fault
occurs, the CPU traps the faulting

24
00:01:53.976 --> 00:02:00.672
instruction, saves the instruction pointer and
registers, and transfers control to the kernel

25
00:02:00.672 --> 00:02:01.187
handler.

26
00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:08.515
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

27
00:02:08.515 --> 00:02:14.187
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

28
00:02:14.306 --> 00:02:22.051
Next, compare the choices involved in Page
Tables & Virtual Memory and make the decision

29
00:02:22.051 --> 00:02:23.084
criteria explicit.

30
00:02:23.084 --> 00:02:29.756
Page tables map virtual addresses to physical
memory with permission bits.

31
00:02:29.756 --> 00:02:37.126
Consider this worked case: A page table entry
for a read-only code segment contains a valid

32
00:02:37.126 --> 00:02:41.271
bit, a present bit, and a read-only permission
flag.

33
00:02:41.271 --> 00:02:48.235
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

34
00:02:48.235 --> 00:02:49.396
the goalposts.

35
00:02:49.396 --> 00:02:56.302
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

36
00:02:56.426 --> 00:03:03.478
Apply Processor Scheduling to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

37
00:03:03.478 --> 00:03:04.563
isolated definition.

38
00:03:04.563 --> 00:03:10.766
The scheduler selects runnable processes and
switches processor context.

39
00:03:10.766 --> 00:03:18.059
Consider this worked case: The OS scheduler
picks a ready process from the run queue,

40
00:03:18.059 --> 00:03:23.407
saves the current process's registers, and
loads the new process's context.

41
00:03:23.407 --> 00:03:31.097
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

42
00:03:31.097 --> 00:03:31.610
step.

43
00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:36.547
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

44
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:42.880
Examine the tradeoffs in Locks &
Synchronization, including what improves and

45
00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.146
what becomes more difficult.

46
00:03:45.146 --> 00:03:51.146
Locks protect invariants when concurrent
execution can access shared state.

47
00:03:51.146 --> 00:03:58.809
Consider this worked case: A mutex lock
ensures that only one thread can modify a

48
00:03:58.809 --> 00:04:02.896
shared counter at a time, preventing race
conditions.

49
00:04:02.896 --> 00:04:09.771
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

50
00:04:09.771 --> 00:04:17.365
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

51
00:04:17.365 --> 00:04:24.144
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

52
00:04:24.144 --> 00:04:26.178
throughout the analysis.

53
00:04:26.282 --> 00:04:33.635
Stress-test the reasoning around Deadlock &
Resource Management with an edge case and

54
00:04:33.635 --> 00:04:37.028
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

55
00:04:37.028 --> 00:04:45.231
Analysis distinguishes user mode, privileged
kernel code, hardware translation, interrupt

56
00:04:45.231 --> 00:04:50.153
state, resource ownership, and synchronization
invariants.

57
00:04:50.153 --> 00:04:58.397
Consider this worked case: Two processes each
hold one file descriptor and wait for the

58
00:04:58.397 --> 00:05:02.794
other's, creating a circular dependency that
halts progress.

59
00:05:02.794 --> 00:05:09.216
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

60
00:05:09.216 --> 00:05:16.200
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

61
00:05:16.322 --> 00:05:24.145
Use Multiprogramming & Multiprocessing to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

62
00:05:24.145 --> 00:05:26.990
causes, and corrective action.

63
00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:32.554
Topics include process synchronization,
interprocess communication, deadlock,

64
00:05:32.554 --> 00:05:38.912
multiprogramming and multiprocessing,
processor scheduling and resource management,

65
00:05:38.912 --> 00:05:46.860
memory management, static and dynamic
relocation, virtual memory, file systems,

66
00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:56.398
logical and physical I/O, device allocation,
I/O processor scheduling, process and resource

67
00:05:56.398 --> 00:05:57.193
protection.

68
00:05:57.193 --> 00:06:04.337
Consider this worked case: A server handles
multiple client requests simultaneously by

69
00:06:04.337 --> 00:06:09.100
switching between processes on a single CPU
core.

70
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:16.704
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

71
00:06:16.704 --> 00:06:18.334
smallest plausible cause.

72
00:06:18.334 --> 00:06:25.912
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

73
00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:33.438
Connect File Systems & I/O to the earlier
ideas and explain how the pieces work as one

74
00:06:33.438 --> 00:06:33.874
system.

75
00:06:33.874 --> 00:06:40.920
The file system organizes persistent data
through inodes, directories, and logged

76
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:41.561
updates.

77
00:06:41.561 --> 00:06:49.146
Consider this worked case: A directory entry
points to an inode that stores file metadata,

78
00:06:49.146 --> 00:06:52.686
permissions, and block addresses for data
storage.

79
00:06:52.686 --> 00:07:01.914
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

80
00:07:01.914 --> 00:07:03.452
across representations.

81
00:07:03.452 --> 00:07:10.842
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

82
00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:17.748
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

83
00:07:17.748 --> 00:07:18.232
analysis.

84
00:07:18.232 --> 00:07:24.994
Architecture analysis preserves data
dependencies, control dependencies, and

85
00:07:24.994 --> 00:07:26.685
architectural state.

86
00:07:26.685 --> 00:07:33.282
Consider this worked case: Audit a kernel
module to ensure all shared variables are

87
00:07:33.282 --> 00:07:38.466
protected by locks and that page table
permissions match access rights.

88
00:07:38.466 --> 00:07:46.727
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

89
00:07:46.727 --> 00:07:47.998
verification step.

90
00:07:47.998 --> 00:07:54.851
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

91
00:07:54.851 --> 00:07:55.341
